  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.4. Increased capacity of constituents to provide adequate labour protection to workers in diverse forms of work arrangements, including on digital labour platforms, and in informal employment</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Two studies on informality in the context of COVID-19 provide evidence for action: 1) an informality and vulnerability diagnostic among disadvantaged groups prepared through a survey and Focus Group Discussions (FGD); 2) A rapid diagnostic on informality to assess the impact of several socio-economic crises, including COVID-19 on the Lebanese economy, in particular informal economy workers and enterprises. 
Concerning the informality and vulnerability diagnostic, as of October 2021, Lebanon has an evidence-based study published. With the recent formation of a new government in September 2021, a tripartite policy discussion and validation workshop was held in December 2021. A policy note with policy recommendations based on the study results forms the basis for the policy dialogue that should support the design of a roadmap to promote formalization and address vulnerability. The social partners were consulted during the study and participated in FGDs.
Concerning the rapid diagnostic on informality, a policy brief and a video were developed and published alongside the full report. The study provides findings about major components of informal employment, e.g. that while 92 per cent of all enterprises, including own-account workers, report being registered, only 10 per cent offered written employment contracts, and nearly 50 per cent do not offer any non-pay benefits (e.g. social security, health insurance, paid holidays, etc.) to their workers highlighting the prevalence of informal employment in formal sector enterprises. The ILO in collaboration with UNESCO and UN Women developed a joint programme to support the impacted enterprises including job creation, formalization and productivity. The programme will be discussed with the government newly established and social partners. In the meantime, findings were discussed and validated through FGDs (August 2021, with 27 business owners to further examine the current state of their businesses. With the support of the Ford Foundation, the ILO commissioned in 2019 Statistics Lebanon to conduct a survey and FGDs on informality and vulnerability in Lebanon to inform the diagnosis of informality. The ILO worked closely with Statistics Lebanon on the survey questionnaire, FGDs guide and the sampling frame. The ILO has also provided training to enumerators. Once data received, the ILO reviewed results, worked on the finalization of the report and translation (all completed in August 2021).
The ILO has also commissioned a video on informality in Lebanon (finalized in May 2021). The ILO organized a first webinar on 2nd September 2021 to launch the report, present its findings and recommendations, with a follow-up policy dialogue with relevant tripartite stakeholders. The policy note prepared by the ILO providing detailed recommendations was discussed with tripartite constituents during this policy dialogue that took place in December 2021.
Concerning the rapid diagnostic of the impact of COVID-19 on the informal economy, the ILO in collaboration with Fafo institute conducted a study on 1,664 businesses classified as Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises (MSMEs) located in the areas surrounding the explosion site to assess the impact from the multiple crisis, including the COVID-19. The study mapped out the level of informality of the enterprises and workers, the impacts and their coping measures. 
The ILO contributed directly to the definition of the methodology, the realization of the assessment, the review of analysis and results and the finalization of the report, the process of consultation to validate the findings and recommendations.</narrative>
   </description>
